<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>🌧️ LIVE PRECIPITATION</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> 9:55PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> 9:55PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE PRECIPITATION</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LIVE / CURRENT OBSERVATION (MRMS 2-min QC mosaic)</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/precipitation/legend.png?v=mrms-qc-20261003-015440" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Precipitation intensity (MRMS quality-controlled composite reflectivity, dBZ, OBSERVED radar echo): <b>LOWEST 5 Very Light</b> cyan/blue (drizzle, mist) &rarr; <b>Light</b> greens (~20-30: light rain/snow) &rarr; <b>Moderate</b> dark green (~30-40: steady rain/snow) &rarr; <b>Heavy</b> yellow/orange (~40-50: heavy rain) &rarr; <b>Severe</b> red (~50-60: severe storms, hail possible) &rarr; <b>Intense</b> magenta/purple (~60-70: violent storms, large hail) &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 70+ Extreme</b> white (destructive hail, debris). Same dBZ always shows the same color in every season — rain, snow, lake-effect snow, thunderstorms. The quality-controlled mosaic filters non-precipitation echoes (ground clutter, migrating birds/insects), so clear air stays see-through and only real precipitation paints.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NOAA NCEP MRMS MergedReflectivityQC quality-controlled composite reflectivity (~2-minute files)<br/><a href="https://mrms.ncep.noaa.gov/2D/MergedReflectivityQC/">https://mrms.ncep.noaa.gov/2D/MergedReflectivityQC/</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE PRECIPITATION</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> 9:55PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> 9:55PM EDT</p><h2>LIVE PRECIPITATION</h2><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/precipitation/legend.png?v=mrms-qc-20261003-015440" width="600" alt="precipitation gradient key"></p><p>Precipitation intensity (MRMS quality-controlled composite reflectivity, dBZ, OBSERVED radar echo): <b>LOWEST 5 Very Light</b> cyan/blue (drizzle, mist) &rarr; <b>Light</b> greens (~20-30: light rain/snow) &rarr; <b>Moderate</b> dark green (~30-40: steady rain/snow) &rarr; <b>Heavy</b> yellow/orange (~40-50: heavy rain) &rarr; <b>Severe</b> red (~50-60: severe storms, hail possible) &rarr; <b>Intense</b> magenta/purple (~60-70: violent storms, large hail) &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ 70+ Extreme</b> white (destructive hail, debris). Same dBZ always shows the same color in every season — rain, snow, lake-effect snow, thunderstorms. The quality-controlled mosaic filters non-precipitation echoes (ground clutter, migrating birds/insects), so clear air stays see-through and only real precipitation paints.</p><p>How to read it in any season. <b>Light</b> greens are light rain in summer — and light snow in winter. <b>Moderate</b> dark green is steady rain or steady snow, including lake-effect bands. <b>Heavy</b> yellow/orange is heavy rain (or very heavy snow rates). <b>Severe</b> red means severe thunderstorms with hail possible; <b>Intense</b> magenta/purple means violent storms with large hail and flash flooding; <b>Extreme</b> white is reserved for the most destructive echoes. Radar sees precipitation particles, not air temperature, so one scale covers rain, snow, and thunderstorms all year. Where you see through the overlay, there is no precipitation right now.</p><p><b>Variable:</b> MergedReflectivityQC composite reflectivity (dBZ): quality-controlled radar echo strength — rain, snow, and thunderstorms in all seasons with ground clutter and biological scatter filtered out; echo intensity, not type.<br/><b>Units:</b> dBZ<br/><b>Source:</b> NOAA NCEP MRMS MergedReflectivityQC quality-controlled composite reflectivity (~2-minute files)<br/><b>Update:</b> QC files about every 2 minutes; Google Earth re-polls every 30 seconds<br/><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://mrms.ncep.noaa.gov/2D/MergedReflectivityQC/">NOAA NCEP MRMS</a></p>]]></description>
